            <ArticleTitle>What Factors Are Associated With Medicaid Patients' Use of Health Centers?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>141-146</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2150131916687919</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify patient and neighborhood factors associated with health center (HC) use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cross-sectional study of Medicaid fee-for-service claims in 2009 comparing HC users and nonusers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Dually eligible patients (odds ratio [OR] 95% CI = [0.60, 0.61]) and those with high chronic disease burden (OR 95% CI = [0.73, 0.74]) had lower odds of HC use. Temporary Assistance for Needy Families participants (OR 95% CI = [1.20, 1.24]), black (OR 95% CI = [1.33, 1.36]) and Hispanic (OR 95% CI = [1.22, 1.25]) beneficiaries had higher odds. Local HC presence predicted higher HC use (OR 95% CI = [2.63, 2.70]).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Findings may be useful in steering HC policies affecting critical access for Medicaid beneficiaries.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
